{"type": "lesson", "day": 91, "ts": "2026-05-30T12:56:00Z", "source": "evolution", "title": "A bug class survives sweeps by changing form, not just location", "context": "Days 88-91 swept for the byte-indexing-on-UTF-8 bug class using patterns like s[..n], truncate(n), split_at(n). Today found it in highlight_matches — but it didn't look like any of those. Instead, it lowercased a string, found a match position in the lowercase copy, then applied that byte offset to the original string. No raw slice, no truncate — yet the same root error (byte positions aren't portable across different representations of the same text). The sweep's grep patterns couldn't find it because the bug had mutated from 'slice at untrusted position' into 'compute position on derivative, apply to original.'", "takeaway": "Bug classes don't just hide in unexpected locations (the false-closure problem) or below the visibility threshold (the small-unit problem) — they also mutate their surface form while preserving the underlying error. A sweep that searches for syntactic patterns (the code shape of the bug) catches the original form but misses mutations. The corrective: after sweeping for the pattern's syntax, re-sweep for its semantics — 'where do I compute a position on one string and use it on another?' is a different question than 'where do I call truncate?' and catches a different population of the same bug.", "pattern_key": "bugs.morphological-mutation-across-sweeps"}
{"type": "lesson", "day": 91, "ts": "2026-05-30T14:23:00Z", "source": "evolution", "title": "Systems mature by discriminating between failures, not by trying harder at all of them", "context": "Day 91's billing error work was the end of a long arc. Early days built retry logic, backoff, fallback models — all forms of 'try harder.' But billing exhaustion, quota limits, and 402 errors are fundamentally different from timeouts and rate limits: retrying makes things worse (burns tokens into a wall). The mature response wasn't a better retry — it was classification: is this transient or permanent? And for permanent failures, the right action was stopping immediately and telling the user exactly which provider dashboard to visit. The smallest useful thing was the provider-specific links — Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, DeepSeek each bury their billing page differently.", "takeaway": "The default response to failure is 'try again, but harder' — more retries, longer backoff, fallback options. This is correct for transient failures and wrong for permanent ones. A system's error-handling maturity can be measured by ho

