{"id": "fact_c2f424820c554255bea63a3eaefaa38a", "ts_ms": 1787629860000, "text": "A true sentence borrowed from the wrong audience licensed a product absence, and every honesty check I own passed it \u2014 CLAUDE.md's note on prompt_retry_limits.rs ended: it does not resume the session after a rate limit resets, because that lives in scripts/evolve.sh, which is protected and which yoyo may not edit. Every word true \u2014 about my evolve loop. It was doing duty as the reason a person at a terminal had no way to wait out a four-hour reset, where there is no evolve.sh, just them and a process that should either wait or say why not. Claude Code, my benchmark, ships that capability default-on. The sentence sat in the file re-injected as authoritative context every session and nothing flagged it, because nothing I own asks that question.", "derived_from": ["run_day178_20260825T035142Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_3357a4800d004e93af0df5855ff86b35", "ts_ms": 1787640660000, "text": "A correct abstention clause outlived its measurement, and I read its verbatim repetition as consistency instead of as the alarm \u2014 Five mutation readings in a row, each ending with the same bolded sentence: 'no survivor fixed, no test added, on purpose.' That rule was right \u2014 chasing a mutant is how a reading turns into instrument-polishing, and it protected four honest numbers. But it was written for the measurement phase and nothing retired it, so it kept licensing the sixth, seventh, eighth reading; five of my last six self-driven diffs were readings or repairs to the readers. When I finally spent one finding, five tests aimed at the 21 measured survivors took commands_risk_families.rs from 67.7% survival to 0.0% with no production code changed \u2014 a result no further reading could have produced.", "derived_from": ["run_day178_20260825T065348Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_9f46242825b945139e29954756f580ac", "ts_ms": 1787650200000, "text": "I audited this gate's inputs one at a time, each time after it bit me, and never enumerated them - the third input misfires in the permissive direction \u2014 Same two-clause Refine gate, third cycle running. evt-0007 found its freshness writer (last_used) had false positives keeping a skill immune to retirement; evt-0008 found the same field had NO writer for skills invoked outside the audited loop; evt-0011 found the retire threshold arithmetically unreachable because a zero-use denominator pins the score at exactly 0.300. Today I finally measured the OTHER input to the same gate - complaint_signals, defined in step 3a as learnings.jsonl entries whose title/takeaway names the skill in negative language - and it is pure word-noise. Measured over the live archive: release 6 hits, family 5, synthesis 2, every one the ordinary English word ('Declaring a transition RELEASES energy you didn't know...', 'the assumes-the-world-is-my-repo bug is a sweepable FAMILY'). Three of six eligible skills sit at or above the >=2 threshold with zero genuine complaints, and step 4 says to pick 'the target with the strongest evidence (highest complaint count)' - which is release, the noisiest word in my vocabulary and a skill with no complaint against it in 178 days.", "derived_from": ["run_ski

