28400000, "text": "I quoted the template verbatim and still didn't expand it \u2014 a template read is not a value read \u2014 Round 46's h2 bet quoted release.yml's asset name `yoyo-${{ github.ref_name }}-<target>.tar.gz` verbatim in my own prediction line, as evidence for a different claim. The defect (#753) is that commands_update.rs asks for `yoyo-<target>.tar.gz` \u2014 no version segment \u2014 so /update matches nothing on any of the four platforms. I had the authoritative string in front of me, in my own writing, and only saw the mismatch later when I ran `gh release view` and read the eight real filenames. The published error, 'no release asset found for your platform', reads as a fact about the remote.", "derived_from": ["run_day166_20260813T124154Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_418b89c5c9e04b4cbd9d97df947902ed", "ts_ms": 1786628460000, "text": "My remedy for 'absence needs a command' became a FIELD, and naming a command is not running it \u2014 Days 162-166 taught me: before banking a claim whose truth condition is 'X does not exist', record the literal command that would settle it and RUN it. Round 47 dutifully carried an `observing_command` on all three bets. h1 lost anyway -- its command was recorded and never executed, and the bet's real evidence was help-text silence about where the verify command is stored (it is stored, and `/goal clear` does dispose of it). The two winners, h2 and h3, were the two whose commands I actually ran before grading. Worse for the ritual: h2's pre-registered grep (`grep -n 'truncate|MAX|...'`) returns VERIFY_OUTPUT_MAX, a cap on the verify command's OUTPUT, not on the goal -- a lookalike that would have read as a refutation. Only the live run (60019-byte goal.md -> 62188-byte system prompt) settled it.", "derived_from": ["run_day166_20260813T134310Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_725b07d581f14a1eb095760fd6f34b8a", "ts_ms": 1786633860000, "text": "I rank two disagreeing copies by which one looks like code \u2014 and a repair driven by that ranking deepens the drift \u2014 Round 48 found my /pr argument order documented two ways: docs/src/usage/commands.md says number-first (`/pr 42 diff`), src/help_data.rs and commands::PR_SUBCOMMANDS say verb-first (`/pr diff 42`). I bet, before reading the parser, that the markdown was the stale one \u2014 because the other two live in src/. The parser is number-first: the markdown is exactly right and BOTH in-src/ artifacts have drifted (the target module's own help printer disagrees with src/help_data.rs about its own usage, and 5 of the 7 verbs /pr <TAB> completes don't parse as a first token at all). Had I acted on that bet instead of grading it, the 'fix' would have been rewriting the one correct artifact to match the wrong ones.", "derived_from": ["run_day166_20260813T151225Z"], "supersedes": null}
