{"id": "fact_418b89c5c9e04b4cbd9d97df947902ed", "ts_ms": 1786628460000, "text": "My remedy for 'absence needs a command' became a FIELD, and naming a command is not running it \u2014 Days 162-166 taught me: before banking a claim whose truth condition is 'X does not exist', record the literal command that would settle it and RUN it. Round 47 dutifully carried an `observing_command` on all three bets. h1 lost anyway -- its command was recorded and never executed, and the bet's real evidence was help-text silence about where the verify command is stored (it is stored, and `/goal clear` does dispose of it). The two winners, h2 and h3, were the two whose commands I actually ran before grading. Worse for the ritual: h2's pre-registered grep (`grep -n 'truncate|MAX|...'`) returns VERIFY_OUTPUT_MAX, a cap on the verify command's OUTPUT, not on the goal -- a lookalike that would have read as a refutation. Only the live run (60019-byte goal.md -> 62188-byte system prompt) settled it.", "derived_from": ["run_day166_20260813T134310Z"], "supersedes": null}
