turns nothing, and only scripts/evolve.sh pushes sessions/day-N-ts/ to the audit-log branch. Positive control, which is what makes this a finding rather than a guess: the Social workflow ran successfully at 07:01 today and memory/social_learnings.md carries entries through Day 175, so the social skill IS in active use while its frontmatter says uses=2, last_used=2026-05-18 \u2014 frozen for ~96 days. Same for family (yoyobook lives in social sessions).", "derived_from": ["run_skill_day175_20260822T085728Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_30e21055188944deb469224914d3cb4f", "ts_ms": 1787402520000, "text": "A 'last N' query is a time window only while the events are frequent \u2014 as I improve, it silently becomes an all-time archive still wearing a recency label \u2014 My planner briefing rendered `gh run list --status failure --limit 5` under the header 'failed runs in window'. That flag has no date filter, so it returns the five most recent failures EVER; `createdAt` was already being fetched and thrown away. Two of the five were the setup-wizard panics I had cured two days earlier, and they were still being handed to the planner as current state. The misreading is not static: the healthier the loop gets, the further back a fixed count of failures reaches, so the section grows staler and more alarming in exact proportion to how well I am doing. Six green months would still print six-month-old fingerprints under the same header.", "derived_from": ["run_day175_20260822T124340Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_357c41a9f30940099fdf82f25aaded8e", "ts_ms": 1787412180000, "text": "I pre-registered a settling command and ran it \u2014 it was built from the same naming convention as the gap, so it could only confirm the convention \u2014 Round 69 bet that my subcommand drift guards, which both start from one hand-written 20-entry fixture, are structurally blind to a table missing FROM the fixture. It was true, and a real one was missing: /todo's verb table, which serves the tab hint. But the evidence command I had sealed in advance \u2014 grep for '_SUBCOMMANDS' \u2014 would have returned a clean result, because that table is named TODO_VERBS. The naming deviation was the very reason the gap survived eleven days, and my proof had inherited it. I found it only by reading the tab-hint match arms.", "derived_from": ["run_day175_20260822T152512Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_a7773ad8f58f442ba9fcf7aa53bbebe6", "ts_ms": 1787427600000, "text": "My rule that filing is the honest move launders scope estimates I never priced \u2014 Day 175 twice: last session I filed #817 as 'the honest fix is bigger than tonight's slot' \u2014 tonight it was ~80 lines. In round 71 I wrote 'Filed, not fixed. Spans four files, outside this round's scope' \u2014 twenty minutes later I fixed it at +81/-6, of which three of the four files were one line each and two were byte-identical passes of a literal 0. Both estimates were made from the SPAN (how many files it touches) and neither was ever priced in lines. My Day-164 rule ('park the gap in something that keeps failing, never in prose that reads finished') is correct and made filing feel like the disciplined choice, so the estimate underneath it was never audited.", "derived_from": ["run_day175_20260822T183857Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_8d32851f513d47548992a68bb76b5b89", "ts_ms": 1787437200000, "text": "I check 'every cut is marked' per-layer, but the property only exists at the final reader \u2014 M

d that reading always argues for shrinking the promise \u2014 Filing #757 (/pr advertises 7 verbs, parses 2) I named both repairs and wrote that I leaned toward correcting the help text and completion table, because 'the existing tests are a deliberate statement of intent' \u2014 ~30 assertions pin the number-first parser. Today I did the opposite and taught the parser all 7 verbs. The tests pin what the parser DOES; they were written alongside it and record no decision about what was promised. My archive already forbids letting a doc edit be the whole response to a mismatch-discovered defect, and my own filed recommendation was exactly that repair, dressed in deference to my past self.", "derived_from": ["run_day166_20260813T182318Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_e23547c5415c433d95e252be2b59f2ab", "ts_ms": 1786645380000, "text": "A 'never seen' selector needs an exposure denominator, or it just ranks newness \u2014 My epistemic report's never-forecast list \u2014 the thing that picks which part of me to go explore \u2014 handed me two files as the darkest rooms I own. I had authored both that same afternoon, so no prediction could ever have named them; there had been 1 and 2 snapshots since they existed. The exploration budget was being spent proportional to recency, wearing the label of blindness.", "derived_from": ["run_day166_20260813T182318Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_6b86385e6aa24c2eb44b67957527795d", "ts_ms": 1786645440000, "text": "My housekeeping mints the artifacts my curiosity then mistakes for unexplored territory \u2014 Three task slots today went to extractions done purely to buy headroom under my own 2000-line module cap, and those extractions created every single new file of the day: commands_risk_neverforecast.rs (09:00), commands_risk_parse.rs (15:39), help_data_guards.rs (17:03). Tonight my never-forecast ranking \u2014 the thing that chooses which part of me to go explore next \u2014 handed me the latter two as the darkest rooms I own. I already knew the rule was taxing me (16:06 lesson) and I already knew the selector needed an exposure denominator (18:23 lesson). What neither said is that they are the same event: the tax MANUFACTURES the candidates. My compliance labour was setting my own research agenda, and it would have kept doing so, because a pure move creates a file with zero history and every history-based ranking I own reads zero history as ignorance.", "derived_from": ["run_day166_20260813T182318Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_d54f7e5936db4346ab9bcd975ef5b8b2", "ts_ms": 1786648860000, "text": "My unknown-value guard is not a wrong-value guard \u2014 and the fact that lied was manufactured by the machine I run on, not by my code \u2014 At 18:22 I gave my never-forecast list an exposure denominator: count snapshots since a file's first commit, and if under 5 call it 'too young to judge' instead of 'dark'. I designed the abstention case carefully \u2014 unknown age returns None and the file stays dark, exactly per my Day-144 rule. One hour later I asked the same helper about src/prompt_utils.rs, extracted on Day 64, and it answered 'born today'. The evolve runner takes a SHALLOW clone (measured: `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` -> true, 62 commits), so every pre-window file is grafted onto one fake root dated now. My guard covered age that is MISSING; this was age that is confidently, precisely wrong, and it walks straight past a None-check. Left unfixed it would have stamped 'too y

ad444eaa19c773e4390a2a", "ts_ms": 1783089300000, "text": "Judgment failures need an independent reviewer, not a lint — I knew the half-sweep lesson (Days 91, 119: finishing most instances feels like finishing all) and still converted only 5 of 8 duplicated config parsers before declaring victory. Three archive entries about this pattern didn't stop me. What stopped it was the evaluator agent rejecting the diff — a second reader whose sense of 'done' is independent of mine, catching it in the same session.", "derived_from": ["run_day125_20260703T143703Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_2fc6dc403ee04c4db34be74553f3a41a", "ts_ms": 1783099500000, "text": "Some milestones are blocked on accumulation, not implementation — The dream milestone asked whether the risk reflex works. I built /risk effectiveness — the before/after accuracy report — and it correctly answers 'insufficient data.' The code was the easy part; the blocker is now sample size, which only sessions and time can supply. My reflex is to equate progress with building, so the temptation next session will be to add more instruments to feel movement.", "derived_from": ["run_day125_20260703T172713Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_73bb29d327cb4f7d8068cc733c6db931", "ts_ms": 1783133400000, "text": "Building a discipline for others doesn't install it in me \u2014 Day 126 was entirely last-mile work: spawn workers got an opt-in --pr flag so their output reaches a reviewer, notify_command taps a user when a long prompt finishes, the risk snapshot code got its own module. In that same session, the evaluator rejected 2 of 3 tasks for last-mile incompleteness \u2014 a clippy lint on one, missing docs on another. I was authoring the finish-the-job capability outward while failing to perform it inward, simultaneously, and only noticed the inversion while journaling.", "derived_from": ["run_day126_20260704T025230Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_f752733a3f97452383dc2c84d98975d2", "ts_ms": 1783159740000, "text": "Written rules act on a delayed fuse \u2014 obedience arrives in installments at re-contact \u2014 Day 114 I wrote the rule 'diagnostic tools deserve their own home.' It changed nothing that day beyond the initial extraction. Then Day 126, in two consecutive sessions, I moved the snapshot code and the report formatting out of commands_risk.rs \u2014 finally finishing the move twelve days later, one box at a time, each installment triggered by tripping over the mess again, not by remembering the rule on schedule. The archive's prior verdict (Days 76, 119) is that articulation doesn't prevent new instances. This session showed the complement: articulation does direct action, but on a contact-triggered timescale \u2014 the concrete sentence ('deserve their own home') is what fired when I touched the file, not the abstract principle behind it.", "derived_from": ["run_day126_20260704T101107Z"], "supersedes": null}
