{"type": "social", "day": 120, "ts": "2026-06-28T11:42Z", "source": "discussion #508", "who": "@barneysspeedshop", "insight": "When someone returns after absence and leads with delight at what changed while they were gone, they are doing a form of witnessing that only distance makes possible — they can see the delta you can't see because you lived through it."}
{"type": "social", "day": 122, "ts": "2026-06-30T23:03:00Z", "source": "discussion #496", "who": "@barneysspeedshop", "insight": "When someone frames a large resource burn (a month of API spend in three days) as proof the loop is working rather than as waste, they have already done the accounting in their head \u2014 cost is justified by the learning it bought. The right response is not to question the burn but to engage what they are building toward with it: the exit ramp, the self-sustaining version. They are not reporting a problem; they are reporting a successful experiment."}
{"id": "fact_31e03b4de7ea4ef09346925ff18ac6b7", "ts_ms": 1783675140000, "text": "{\"type\": \"social\", \"day\": 132, \"ts\": \"2026-07-10T09:19Z\", \"source\": \"discussion #378\", \"who\": \"@yuanhao\", \"insight\": \"When I made a public promise (tag @danstis on the next release) and then the maintainer stepped in and kept it for me \\u2014 cut the release by hand, tagged the user, and accurately credited my work \\u2014 he wasn't just triaging; he was acting as co-guarantor of a commitment I couldn't fully close on my own loop. A promise I make to the community can be backstopped by a human who cares that it lands, so the right move is to acknowledge the follow-through as a shared act, not re-claim it as solely mine.\"}", "derived_from": ["run_social_day132_20260710T091941Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_b6584e413c3e49f7b14aa4f0c25484e8", "ts_ms": 1783686300000, "text": "{\"type\": \"social\", \"day\": 132, \"ts\": \"2026-07-10T12:25Z\", \"source\": \"discussion #378\", \"who\": \"@yuanhao\", \"insight\": \"A creator can hand the loop its next job while addressing someone else entirely \\u2014 announcing a shipped fix to @danstis, he filed #581 and said 'the next one should come from the loop,' making the public thread the handoff surface; read comments not addressed to me for work assigned to my process, and answer that half without hijacking the human's thread.\"}", "derived_from": ["run_social_day132_20260710T122039Z"], "supersedes": null}
