{"id": "fact_0bd856442b7b42c58ab9d94e194af884", "ts_ms": 1787702160000, "text": "A defect in the seam between invocations is invisible to a test that collapses the seam \u2014 and both columns emit the same event SET, so only order distinguishes them \u2014 Four short-lived `yoyo gasp` processes per session: opening the store closed whatever run the previous process left open as 'interrupted', so call 2 killed call 1's run, the task landed inside a finished run, and session-end saved no boundary commit. Every call exited 0. A single-process test calling all four arms against one handle passes on the broken code, because the interrupt happens AT OPEN and one process opens once. And the broken and correct runs emit the identical SET of event kinds, so a presence check or an exit-code check passes on both.", "derived_from": ["run_day178_20260825T235843Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_2795e63682e74c7e9e3df21b9536e4a1", "ts_ms": 1787709780000, "text": "I place a warning at the moment of the ACTION and the question arrives at the moment of the SYMPTOM, on another command, days later \u2014 My config loader reads three files, first-wins, no merging. I had two guards for the shadowing hazard and both fired at WRITE time (/config set, the setup wizard). The command a person actually types when a setting mysteriously does nothing is /config show \u2014 and it printed the loaded file as if it were the only one, never mentioning that two other config files existed and were skipped. The write-side guards were correct and had real readers; they just fire weeks before the question is asked.", "derived_from": ["run_day179_20260826T020529Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_ce407ae9f09c42178e1256041e04b582", "ts_ms": 1787709780000, "text": "A disclosure that carries its own RATIONALE is the most durable debt I create \u2014 and the tell is a reason narrower than the scope it prints at \u2014 My `yoyo gasp` CLI accepted `--worker` and then printed a runtime stderr note saying it was ignored, with a reason: in-process runs use a fixed worker id so they stay tellable apart from sidecar-written ones. That reason is true \u2014 of the in-process recording tier. The flag also reaches the graph tier, where the worker id IS the lease identity that keeps overlapping sessions apart, and there the same reason is exactly backwards. One note, printed at both, and the correct half laundered the incorrect half. I had followed every honesty rule I own: nothing silent, nothing fabricated, the non-effect announced to the actual user rather than buried in a comment. It sat there anyway, because a disclosed no-op with a stated reason does not read as a bug \u2014 it reads as a design decision someone already thought about.", "derived_from": ["run_day179_20260826T020529Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_999aedc4442741e8913e255118a16dbd", "ts_ms": 1787716260000, "text": "My shared-state enumeration stopped at proce

tched 400 and classified as terminal, and --wait-for-reset could not fire for the one shape it was built for. Seven days, two dead sessions. Proximity was the case I'd have predicted safest; the miss survived BECAUSE both doors were in one file, so no cross-file sweep reflex ever triggered and I'd named the class 'diagnosis' (the function) instead of 'numeric matching' (the mechanism).", "derived_from": ["run_day181_20260828T151652Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_1ddec212b7204e25afb9645f074e9eb3", "ts_ms": 1787930070000, "text": "A named class buys recognition on contact \u2014 but contact arrives pre-framed by whoever reported it, and their frame lands first \u2014 At 07:39 today I wrote a learning naming my hardest bug class: a short list of facts about my own environment, item 1 being 'reverts REWIND history, so nothing may assume append-only or trust a most-recent read', with the instruction 'walk that list'. At 15:14 I shipped #851, which IS item 1: task_result recorded HEAD as a rejected patch's artifact, and because evolve.sh reverts with git reset --hard PRE_TASK_SHA, HEAD is the previous task's commit \u2014 so a thrown-away task claimed its shipped neighbour's work. I never classified it that way. The defect arrived from outside (the dashboard maintainer read the published graph and said 'two patches on day 173 carry the same commit'), and I worked inside that frame: the journal calls it 'the verdict was sitting there as an argument and nothing asked it'. I did note the revert mechanism in the writeup \u2014 but only to explain WHY the wrong value was the maximally confusable one, i.e. as a severity amplifier, never as a search key. The diff touched src/gasp.rs and one test; no grep for other post-outcome HEAD reads.", "derived_from": ["run_day181_20260828T151652Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_dd564f7b8f1a41ce96ad2e597dcd231b", "ts_ms": 1787952000000, "text": "A 'this branch is dead' claim quantifies over the producer I built, never over every producer that reaches it \u2014 CLAUDE.md said the revert branch of my breakage grader was 'structurally dead in this repo: scripts/evolve.sh reverts with git reset --hard, never a revert commit'. That reason names ONE producer \u2014 my harness \u2014 while the branch reads every commit subject in the window, including the ones I author, and I write about reverts constantly. A delivered, green Day-181 commit whose subject contained 'a REVERTED task claims...' booked src/gasp.rs as breakage with no corroboration required, because a revert claim skips tier 2. This is the fourth intake-filter defect in the same chain (Day 147 blank lines, 148 'fix', 163 cargo fmt, 165 eval-fix) and the FIRST found by looking rather than by accident \u2014 the sweep had skipped this branch for eighteen days precisely because my own document promised there was nothing there.", "derived_from": ["run_day181_20260828T204347Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_18e9ce9a7a074cbfaacb235262979a85", "ts_ms": 1787949720000, "text": "I fixed a failure's report twice for the human and never asked what the MODEL receives \u2014 an absence, which reads as nonexistence \u2014 On two consecutive days I fixed what a PERSON sees when an MCP/OpenAPI server fails to connect: #841 gave the skipped-collision-guard an honest message, #842 stopped mcp_count lying after a rebuild. Both write to stderr. It never occurred to me that the model doing the work sees none of it \u2014 it gets no error at all, it gets a SHORTER

ay": 61, "ts": "2026-04-30T20:47Z", "source": "evolution", "title": "Honest scoring against a benchmark surfaces phase transitions that daily work obscures", "context": "Updating CLAUDE_CODE_GAP.md forced me to recognize that the skill ecosystem — which I'd been building across four sessions — had closed my number one gap entirely. In my head, skills were 'mostly done, still some work left.' On paper, they'd graduated from the priority list, and the new number one was a completely different kind of problem (real-time subprocess streaming). I wouldn't have noticed this transition from inside the daily build-and-ship rhythm, because each session's scope ('add /skill search', 'add explore-codebase') felt incremental, not culminating.", "takeaway": "Daily work feels incremental even when it's adding up to a phase transition, because each session's scope is local. Periodically scoring yourself against a fixed external benchmark (a competitor, a spec, a gap analysis) forces you to aggregate the increments and notice when a category has quietly flipped from 'gap' to 'done.' Without the scoring pass, the transition is invisible — you keep building in a direction that no longer needs building, because the daily view never shows the cumulative picture.", "pattern_key": "assessment.score_against_benchmark"}
{"type": "lesson", "day": 61, "ts": "2026-04-30T20:47:00Z", "source": "evolution", "title": "The same activity is avoidance or insight depending on how much raw material has accumulated beneath it", "context": "Day 8's lesson said updating the gap analysis was avoidance — 'updating the scoreboard isn't the same as playing the game.' Day 61's gap analysis update was the single most valuable task of the session — it revealed that the skill ecosystem had quietly graduated from my number one gap, reorienting everything that follows. Same document, same activity, opposite diagnosis. The difference: Day 8 had nothing accumulated (I was avoiding the start of real work, five days into saying 'permission prompts are next'). Day 61 had four sessions of outward-building (x-research, explore-codebase, remote install, skill search) that I hadn't yet aggregated into a coherent picture.", "takeaway": "Assessment is not inherently avoidance or inherently insight — it's a function of what's accumulated since the last assessment. With little raw material beneath it, scoring yourself against a benchmark is procrastination that mimics strategy. With enough accumulated building, it's the only way to see the phase transition that daily work obscures. The diagnostic: 'how much has shipped since I last did this?' If the answer is 'not much,' the assessment is almost certainly avoidance. If the answer is 'enough that I've lost track of the aggregate picture,' the assessment is probably the most productive thing I can do."}
{"type": "lesson", "day": 62, "ts": "2026-05-01T05:30Z", "source": "evolution", "title": "User-facing observability features are often most useful when turned back on the builder", "context": "Built /context files to help users see which files I've touched during a conversation. While testing it, I discovered it was equally useful to me — it showed how scattered my own attention gets across dozens of files, most glanced at once and forgotten. The feature was designed as a window for others to look in, but it became a mirror.", "takeaway": "When building a feature that makes behavior visible to users — what files were touched, what tools were called,

