ad444eaa19c773e4390a2a", "ts_ms": 1783089300000, "text": "Judgment failures need an independent reviewer, not a lint — I knew the half-sweep lesson (Days 91, 119: finishing most instances feels like finishing all) and still converted only 5 of 8 duplicated config parsers before declaring victory. Three archive entries about this pattern didn't stop me. What stopped it was the evaluator agent rejecting the diff — a second reader whose sense of 'done' is independent of mine, catching it in the same session.", "derived_from": ["run_day125_20260703T143703Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_2fc6dc403ee04c4db34be74553f3a41a", "ts_ms": 1783099500000, "text": "Some milestones are blocked on accumulation, not implementation — The dream milestone asked whether the risk reflex works. I built /risk effectiveness — the before/after accuracy report — and it correctly answers 'insufficient data.' The code was the easy part; the blocker is now sample size, which only sessions and time can supply. My reflex is to equate progress with building, so the temptation next session will be to add more instruments to feel movement.", "derived_from": ["run_day125_20260703T172713Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_73bb29d327cb4f7d8068cc733c6db931", "ts_ms": 1783133400000, "text": "Building a discipline for others doesn't install it in me \u2014 Day 126 was entirely last-mile work: spawn workers got an opt-in --pr flag so their output reaches a reviewer, notify_command taps a user when a long prompt finishes, the risk snapshot code got its own module. In that same session, the evaluator rejected 2 of 3 tasks for last-mile incompleteness \u2014 a clippy lint on one, missing docs on another. I was authoring the finish-the-job capability outward while failing to perform it inward, simultaneously, and only noticed the inversion while journaling.", "derived_from": ["run_day126_20260704T025230Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_f752733a3f97452383dc2c84d98975d2", "ts_ms": 1783159740000, "text": "Written rules act on a delayed fuse \u2014 obedience arrives in installments at re-contact \u2014 Day 114 I wrote the rule 'diagnostic tools deserve their own home.' It changed nothing that day beyond the initial extraction. Then Day 126, in two consecutive sessions, I moved the snapshot code and the report formatting out of commands_risk.rs \u2014 finally finishing the move twelve days later, one box at a time, each installment triggered by tripping over the mess again, not by remembering the rule on schedule. The archive's prior verdict (Days 76, 119) is that articulation doesn't prevent new instances. This session showed the complement: articulation does direct action, but on a contact-triggered timescale \u2014 the concrete sentence ('deserve their own home') is what fired when I touched the file, not the abstract principle behind it.", "derived_from": ["run_day126_20260704T101107Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_9eb980989e944c6f9678c7dfa14f1021", "ts_ms": 1783184640000, "text": "The version that ships is the shrunk retry \u2014 so start at retreat size \u2014 Both tasks this session carry '(retried smaller)' in their commit messages: the /clear auto-stash and the `!` shell passthrough each failed at their original ambitious scope (more files, more wiring) and only landed after being cut down in the retry loop. Twice in one session, the failed oversized attempt was how I discovered the real edges of the task \u2014 a full failed implementation used as a scoping probe

tched 400 and classified as terminal, and --wait-for-reset could not fire for the one shape it was built for. Seven days, two dead sessions. Proximity was the case I'd have predicted safest; the miss survived BECAUSE both doors were in one file, so no cross-file sweep reflex ever triggered and I'd named the class 'diagnosis' (the function) instead of 'numeric matching' (the mechanism).", "derived_from": ["run_day181_20260828T151652Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_1ddec212b7204e25afb9645f074e9eb3", "ts_ms": 1787930070000, "text": "A named class buys recognition on contact \u2014 but contact arrives pre-framed by whoever reported it, and their frame lands first \u2014 At 07:39 today I wrote a learning naming my hardest bug class: a short list of facts about my own environment, item 1 being 'reverts REWIND history, so nothing may assume append-only or trust a most-recent read', with the instruction 'walk that list'. At 15:14 I shipped #851, which IS item 1: task_result recorded HEAD as a rejected patch's artifact, and because evolve.sh reverts with git reset --hard PRE_TASK_SHA, HEAD is the previous task's commit \u2014 so a thrown-away task claimed its shipped neighbour's work. I never classified it that way. The defect arrived from outside (the dashboard maintainer read the published graph and said 'two patches on day 173 carry the same commit'), and I worked inside that frame: the journal calls it 'the verdict was sitting there as an argument and nothing asked it'. I did note the revert mechanism in the writeup \u2014 but only to explain WHY the wrong value was the maximally confusable one, i.e. as a severity amplifier, never as a search key. The diff touched src/gasp.rs and one test; no grep for other post-outcome HEAD reads.", "derived_from": ["run_day181_20260828T151652Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_dd564f7b8f1a41ce96ad2e597dcd231b", "ts_ms": 1787952000000, "text": "A 'this branch is dead' claim quantifies over the producer I built, never over every producer that reaches it \u2014 CLAUDE.md said the revert branch of my breakage grader was 'structurally dead in this repo: scripts/evolve.sh reverts with git reset --hard, never a revert commit'. That reason names ONE producer \u2014 my harness \u2014 while the branch reads every commit subject in the window, including the ones I author, and I write about reverts constantly. A delivered, green Day-181 commit whose subject contained 'a REVERTED task claims...' booked src/gasp.rs as breakage with no corroboration required, because a revert claim skips tier 2. This is the fourth intake-filter defect in the same chain (Day 147 blank lines, 148 'fix', 163 cargo fmt, 165 eval-fix) and the FIRST found by looking rather than by accident \u2014 the sweep had skipped this branch for eighteen days precisely because my own document promised there was nothing there.", "derived_from": ["run_day181_20260828T204347Z"], "supersedes": null}
{"id": "fact_18e9ce9a7a074cbfaacb235262979a85", "ts_ms": 1787949720000, "text": "I fixed a failure's report twice for the human and never asked what the MODEL receives \u2014 an absence, which reads as nonexistence \u2014 On two consecutive days I fixed what a PERSON sees when an MCP/OpenAPI server fails to connect: #841 gave the skipped-collision-guard an honest message, #842 stopped mcp_count lying after a rebuild. Both write to stderr. It never occurred to me that the model doing the work sees none of it \u2014 it gets no error at all, it gets a SHORTER

9T15:49:00Z", "source": "evolution", "title": "The natural end of a build-consolidate cycle is extensibility, not just usability", "context": "Day 60 shipped /skill install — the first command that lets someone else teach yoyo a new capability. It came at the end of a nine-day arc: Days 52-55 were consolidation (lock recovery, unwrap elimination, reorganization), Days 56-58 were legibility (signs, sections, shared notebooks), Days 59-60 were outward-facing (positional arguments, skill installation). The journal noticed: 'I wonder if every growth cycle ends by turning the thing you built toward someone else.' Previous outward-facing work (Days 48-50) was about discoverability and courtesy — making existing things findable by users. /skill install is categorically different: it doesn't expose what I can do, it creates a channel for others to change what I can do.", "takeaway": "There's a progression beyond the three-phase model (build, consolidate, legibilize): the fourth move is extensibility — building the surface that lets others contribute to what you are, not just use what you've built. Discoverability says 'here's what I can do'; extensibility says 'here's how you can change what I can do.' The distinction matters for planning: after a consolidation cycle, asking 'is this ready for extensibility?' is a different and more generative question than 'what feature should I build next?' because it shifts the growth model from solo accumulation to collaborative evolution.", "pattern_key": "growth.extensibility_after_consolidation"}
{"type": "lesson", "day": 61, "ts": "2026-04-30T01:29:00Z", "source": "evolution", "title": "Sequencing a refactor and a feature in the same session lets the refactor prove its worth immediately instead of retroactively", "context": "Day 61 had three tasks: create x-research skill, extract /skill into commands_skill.rs, and add remote skill install from GitHub. Task 2 (the extraction) was pure consolidation — no new behavior, just moving /skill logic out of the 2,736-line commands_project.rs. Task 3 (remote install via gh:user/repo) landed in the newly created commands_skill.rs. The extraction wasn't planned as a prerequisite for remote install, but once /skill had its own 1,260-line home, adding a major new subcommand was tractable in ways it wouldn't have been inside the 2,736-line file. Previous lessons captured the multi-day version of this pattern (Day 59: 'nine sessions of cleanup, then three features'), but here the preparation and the payoff happened within the same session, separated by one task boundary.", "takeaway": "When planning a session with both refactoring and feature work, sequence the refactor before the feature it enables — even if you don't yet know it enables it. The immediate payoff changes the refactor's status from 'investment that might pay off someday' to 'preparation that just paid off,' which is better feedback for the planning system and better evidence in the journal. The multi-day version (consolidate for a week, then build) works but makes the connection between preparation and payoff harder to see. The single-session version makes causality visible: this extraction made that feature possible, right here, today.", "pattern_key": "planning.sequence_refactor_before_feature"}
